lord dieben is the chairman of the  climate change committee and he's  delivering a grim report on government  plans to get to net zero carbon  emissions by 2050.  his verdict  lots of grand promises but nowhere near  enough detail as things stand with the  current program that the government has  net zero will not happen that is true  quite a number of departments have not  yet got into their stride and what we're  saying is we haven't got any time to  wait you know this is very serious it's  no longer possible to ignore the fact  that we will make this planet  uninhabitable if we don't act now  among the major failures identified the  committee describes a shocking gap in  policy for better insulated homes  richard shears already knows this all  too well he made his house more energy  efficient without any government  guidance and now helps others to do the  same it can be confusing for people  starting on the retrofit journey because  there are so many different points of  contact and there's so many different  messages out there that  knowing where to start can be can be a  real headache agriculture and land use  are also singled out for what's  described as glacial progress we really  do want to see that incentivization we  want to see that policy  really help farmers formulate a plan but  i think we do need government to take it  seriously but it's not all bad news  the report says that great progress has  been made on electric vehicles and on  renewable energy but it will make for  very uncomfortable reading for the  government and for boris johnson who as  prime minister has consistently said  that action on climate change is a top  priority  his critics though have never been  convinced  we have a prime minister who essentially  is a climate cheat he's using all the  language to suggest that he's taking  action on climate change but as this  damning report makes so clear he  literally is not doing that there is  this big gulf between his words and his  action  in a statement a government spokesperson  said over the past three decades the uk  has driven down emissions faster than  any other g7 country and we have clear  plans to go further  we are leading the world on climate  change helping over 90 percent of  countries set net zero targets during  our cop 26 presidency up from 30 percent  two years ago  but grabbing the headlines and  delivering on the detail at home are two  very different things and the chasm  between the two must be closed if the  government is to keep its promises on  tackling climate change  hannah thomas peter sky news in  westminster
 

